INT. DEPOSITION ROOM - CONVERTED BOWLING ALLEY - DAY

A long laminate table has been set up across what used to be lane six. Pins are still racked at the far end behind a sheet of clear plastic. A court reporter's machine sits unattended, red light blinking. Two chairs face each other, one pushed in tight, one angled for escape. A pitcher of water sweats between them.

NADIA FERRANTE (40s, attorney, charcoal suit, a legal pad she doesn't need) sits upright, pen capped. Across from her, ELIAS BRANDT (60s, retired shipping executive, rumpled blazer, thick hands folded) leans back like a man in a waiting room.

NADIA
For the record, Mr. Brandt, please state your name.

ELIAS
You know my name.

NADIA
For the record.

ELIAS
Elias Brandt.

NADIA
And you understand you're under oath.

ELIAS
I understand this is a bowling alley.

NADIA
It's the only room the county would lend us.

ELIAS
I used to bowl here. Thursdays.

NADIA
Is that relevant?

ELIAS
Only to me.

She uncaps her pen. She writes the date at the top of the page.

NADIA
Mr. Brandt, on March fourth, did you authorize the transfer of eleven million dollars from the pension fund?

ELIAS
I authorized a loan.

NADIA
To whom?

ELIAS
To the company.

NADIA
Which company?

ELIAS
My company.

NADIA
So you lent yourself eleven million dollars of other people's retirement.

ELIAS
I lent it to the business, Ms. Ferrante. The business employs four hundred people.

NADIA
Employed.

ELIAS
Excuse me?

NADIA
Past tense. Four hundred people were employed. Eleven thousand retirees were promised.

ELIAS
Don't lecture me.

NADIA
I'm not lecturing. I'm asking.

ELIAS
You're performing. There's no jury.

NADIA
There's a record.

ELIAS
Then let it record that the market turned.

NADIA
Mr. Brandt, I'm going to show you Exhibit Nine.

She slides a document across the table. He doesn't touch it.

NADIA (CONT'D)
That's your signature?

ELIAS
Looks like it.

NADIA
Is it?

ELIAS
Yes.

NADIA
And the date next to it?

ELIAS
March fourth.

NADIA
And the note in the margin. Read it aloud, please.

He picks up the paper. He reads in a flat voice.

ELIAS
"Do not tell the trustees until it is done."

Silence. The reporter's light blinks. Down the room, behind the plastic, the pins stand frozen.

NADIA
Why did you write that?

ELIAS
Because they would have said no.

NADIA
Because it was illegal.

ELIAS
Because they were frightened men. Frightened men say no. Somebody had to say yes.

NADIA
Somebody who would profit.

ELIAS
Somebody who would carry it.

NADIA
Eleven thousand people are carrying it now.

ELIAS
(rising, sharply)
Don't you speak to me about carrying! I carried those people for thirty years!

NADIA
Sit down, Mr. Brandt.

ELIAS
I put a roof on every one of them!

NADIA
Sit down.

He slams his palm on the table. The pitcher jumps. Water sloshes over the lip and runs across the laminate, toward the plastic, toward the pins.

Nadia does not flinch. She picks up her pen and writes one line.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
(quieter)
What did you just write?

NADIA
"Witness became agitated."

ELIAS
That's not what happened.

NADIA
Then what happened?

Elias sits. His hand, still wet, trembles on the table. He wipes it on his blazer, slowly, and looks at the lane, at the pins he once knocked down on Thursday nights.

ELIAS
I was happy here.

NADIA
Mr. Brandt, that's not responsive.

ELIAS
I know it isn't.

He lifts his eyes to hers.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
Ask your question again.
